1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:06,170
On a Saturday in March 1932, in his apartment in Paris,

2
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:09,950
one of the most powerful businessmen in the world was found dead.

3
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:12,110
The police ruled it suicide.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,090
His name was Ivar Kreuger.

5
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:17,830
His companies made about two thirds of the world's matches.

6
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:21,990
He had lent nearly four hundred million dollars to some fifteen governments.

7
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:24,990
A few weeks later, accountants opened his books.

8
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:28,390
Profits he had reported for years had never existed.

9
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:33,110
About two hundred and fifty million dollars of his assets had never existed either.

10
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,790
How did a man who sold matches fool banks, investors,

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,950
and whole countries?

12
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:41,290
It started with something every home in the world needed.

13
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:43,950
And with a very good idea about money.

14
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,710
Ivar Kreuger was born in 1880 in Kalmar, a small town in Sweden.

15
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:55,110
His family owned match factories.

16
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:59,850
He studied engineering in Stockholm, and at twenty he left to see the world.

17
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:03,930
For seven years he worked abroad, much of it in the United States.

18
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:09,570
According to a 1933 profile in Fortune, as a young engineer in New York he worked

19
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:11,250
on buildings like the Flatiron.

20
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:16,110
Back home, in 1908, he and a partner, Paul Toll, started a building company.

21
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:19,470
They worked fast, and they worked in a new material:

22
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,050
reinforced concrete.

23
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:25,710
Their firm helped build the stadium for the 1912 Stockholm Olympics.

24
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:28,870
But Kreuger wasn't interested in buildings for long.

25
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:31,590
He was interested in his family's old business.

26
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:32,290
Matches.

27
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:37,930
A match costs almost nothing.

28
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:40,870
But everyone needs them, every single day:

29
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:43,050
for stoves, lamps, cigarettes.

30
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:47,790
Sell enough of them, everywhere, and a tiny profit becomes a fortune.

31
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:52,250
In 1913, Kreuger began buying up Swedish match factories.

32
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:56,610
In 1917, he merged the biggest of them into one company:

33
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:58,230
Swedish Match.

34
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:00,090
He was its president.

35
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:03,410
Then he went looking for money where there was the most of it.

36
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:07,570
In 1923, with a respected Boston bank, Lee, Higginson,

37
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:10,190
he set up a company in the United States:

38
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:11,950
International Match.

39
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:16,070
American investors could now buy a piece of the match empire.

40
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:17,210
They bought.

41
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:22,310
Over the next years, Americans put about two hundred and fifty million dollars into

42
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:23,430
Kreuger's securities.

43
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:27,550
They became some of the most widely held investments in the world.

44
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:34,690
After the First World War, many European governments were broke.

45
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:36,850
Kreuger offered them a deal.

46
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:38,390
I'll lend you dollars.

47
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:42,950
In return, you give my company the right to be your country's only match seller.

48
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:46,950
The first was Poland, in 1925:

49
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:48,210
six million dollars.

50
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:51,210
Then France, in 1927:

51
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:51,730
seventy-five million.

52
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:54,390
In 1929, Germany:

53
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:58,870
one hundred and twenty-five million dollars, the biggest loan of all.

54
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:03,230
In return, Germany kept cheap Russian matches out of the country.

55
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:06,370
And where did the money for those loans come from?

56
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:08,830
Much of it from American investors.

57
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:13,750
To lend to France, Kreuger's American company sold fifty million dollars of its own

58
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:15,270
bonds in the United States.

59
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:18,190
Money from Main Street went to governments in Europe,

60
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:20,630
and came back as interest and monopolies.

61
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:23,070
On paper, it was a perfect circle.

62
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:27,930
Greece, Hungary, Yugoslavia, Romania, Latvia, Ecuador,

63
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:28,630
Turkey…

64
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:34,650
By 1930, he had lent nearly four hundred million dollars to some fifteen governments.

65
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:36,670
And the matches followed.

66
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:42,410
That year, by his own company's count, it controlled about sixty percent of the world's

67
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:44,950
match production, in thirty-three countries.

68
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:47,090
Other estimates were even higher.

69
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:52,050
A man who lends money to entire countries looks like the safest borrower on Earth.

70
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,690
That was the real product Kreuger sold.

71
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:56,050
Not matches.

72
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:56,910
Trust.

73
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:04,350
In 1928, he opened his new headquarters in Stockholm.

74
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:06,830
People called it the Match Palace.

75
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:10,910
He had a penthouse on Park Avenue in New York, with trees on the roof.

76
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:14,470
A twenty-room apartment above Place Vendôme in Paris.

77
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:16,550
Homes in Berlin and Stockholm.

78
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:18,370
A private island.

79
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:23,210
In October 1929, his face was on the cover of Time magazine.

80
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:25,830
It was the same week Wall Street crashed.

81
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:28,010
Almost nobody checked his numbers.

82
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:32,550
When his American bankers asked about audits, they accepted a Swedish one.

83
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:37,610
As Time put it later, they agreed "to accept a Swedish audit of the greatest Swede."

84
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:39,810
And control stayed with him.

85
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:44,070
A small class of voting shares, worth about fifty thousand dollars,

86
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:47,790
mostly his, controlled companies valued at more than a billion.

87
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:54,070
Here is the problem with lending to countries.

88
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:57,310
A government pays you back slowly, over decades.

89
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:01,070
But Kreuger's investors expected big dividends every year.

90
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:05,490
So, from the mid-twenties, the dividends were largely paid not from profits,

91
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:07,930
but from the investors' own capital:

92
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:12,030
new money coming in, paid out to the people who came before.

93
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,930
The money moved through a maze of companies:

94
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:19,870
in Liechtenstein, the Netherlands, and shells with tiny capital and huge debts.

95
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:22,710
Each set of books made the others look healthy.

96
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:25,810
And when he needed collateral, he made it.

97
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:31,150
In his office, Kreuger forged the signatures of Italian officials on government bonds.

98
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:36,550
Fake bonds worth about a hundred million dollars, printed in Stockholm from plates

99
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:37,650
he delivered himself.

100
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:40,710
It worked as long as new money kept arriving.

101
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:44,190
Then the world stopped lending.

102
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:52,130
After the crash of 1929, investors pulled back.

103
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:54,010
Banks wanted their money.

104
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:56,630
Kreuger borrowed from Swedish banks:

105
00:05:56,480 --> 00:06:01,230
about eight hundred million kronor, roughly a tenth of Sweden's entire economy.

106
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:03,490
Then came a deal that went wrong.

107
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:08,750
He had sold control of the telephone maker Ericsson to the American company ITT.

108
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:14,330
In early 1932, ITT discovered the numbers he'd shown them weren't true,

109
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:16,870
and demanded eleven million dollars back.

110
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:18,370
He couldn't pay.

111
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:22,830
On March 12, 1932, he was found dead in his Paris apartment.

112
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:24,390
He left letters.

113
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:29,430
In one, he wrote that he had made such a mess of things that this was the most satisfactory

114
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:30,130
solution.

115
00:06:31,780 --> 00:06:35,310
The Paris police held back the news until the markets closed.

116
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:37,610
Officially, it was suicide.

117
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:40,630
His family always insisted he was murdered.

118
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:42,470
That has never been proven.

119
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:47,010
When the news broke, the Stockholm stock exchange shut its doors.

120
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:50,430
Swedish banks that had lent him money were suddenly in danger,

121
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,410
and the government had to step in.

122
00:06:56,360 --> 00:06:59,510
The accounting firm Price Waterhouse went through his records.

123
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:00,670
Its verdict:

124
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:05,470
the manipulations were so childish that anyone with a basic knowledge of bookkeeping

125
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:06,730
could have seen them.

126
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:11,690
Over fourteen years, he had reported more than three hundred million dollars in profits.

127
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:13,270
The real figure:

128
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:15,150
about forty million.

129
00:07:15,820 --> 00:07:20,030
Along the way, he had taken about five hundred sixty million dollars from investors,

130
00:07:20,170 --> 00:07:23,190
and about one hundred sixty-four million from banks.

131
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:28,010
Kreuger and Toll went bankrupt, the largest bankruptcy on record at that time.

132
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:32,270
When the American receiver opened the accounts, it found less than ten thousand dollars

133
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:33,110
in cash.

134
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:36,510
Creditors got back about thirty-two cents on the dollar.

135
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:41,410
In Sweden, the prime minister resigned after admitting his party had taken Kreuger's

136
00:07:41,260 --> 00:07:41,650
money.

137
00:07:42,180 --> 00:07:44,250
In Washington, the Senate held hearings.

138
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:50,570
The scandal helped shape America's new securities laws of 1933 and 1934,

139
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:53,710
and the idea that public companies need independent audits.

140
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:56,470
The strangest part?

141
00:07:56,940 --> 00:07:58,190
Not everything was fake.

142
00:07:58,660 --> 00:07:59,990
The factories were real.

143
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:03,890
His loan to Germany kept paying interest long after he was gone.

144
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:06,170
And Swedish Match survived.

145
00:08:07,080 --> 00:08:11,671
In 2022, it was bought for about sixteen billion dollars.

146
00:08:12,121 --> 00:08:16,331
That's why a modern historian called him the father of today's financial market.

147
00:08:16,861 --> 00:08:18,151
A dubious honor.

148
00:08:18,901 --> 00:08:21,791
Because what Kreuger really mastered wasn't matches.

149
00:08:22,181 --> 00:08:23,851
It was making people believe.

150
00:08:24,801 --> 00:08:26,171
The matches were real.

151
00:08:26,561 --> 00:08:27,791
The money wasn't.

